---
import LegalLayout from '@/layouts/LegalLayout.astro';
import { SITE } from '@/consts';
---

<LegalLayout
  title="Advertising & affiliate disclosure"
  description="How DevToolSDK makes money, which links are affiliate links, and the specific things we refuse to do — including selling placement inside a guide."
  path="/disclosure"
  lead="Two revenue sources, both disclosed. Neither has ever changed what a guide recommends, and this page explains the controls that make that claim checkable."
>
  <h2 id="summary">The short version</h2>
  <p>
    {SITE.name} makes money from display advertising and from affiliate links. We do not sell
    guides, recommendations, or placement inside a technical instruction. If we ever did, this
    page would say so, because the alternative is lying to you.
  </p>

  <h2 id="advertising">Display advertising</h2>
  <p>
    We serve display ads through Google AdSense. Google sells the inventory and places the ads.
    We cannot see which advertisers bought space on a given page, and advertisers cannot see what
    we are writing or influence it.
  </p>
  <p>
    That separation is the point. A vendor unhappy with what a guide says about their tool has no
    lever to pull — they are not our customer, Google is. Every ad slot on this site is labelled
    "Advertisement", and we deliberately keep no ads above the fold, none on the contact page, and
    the ad load below the level where it starts degrading page performance.
  </p>

  <h2 id="affiliate">Affiliate links</h2>
  <p>
    Some links to the tools we cover are affiliate links. If you click one and later sign up or
    buy, we may receive a commission at no additional cost to you.
  </p>
  <p>Specifically:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Affiliate links carry <code>rel="sponsored nofollow"</code> in the page source, so they are
      machine-identifiable, not just disclosed in prose.
    </li>
    <li>
      A minority of the tools we cover have affiliate programmes at all. Most do not, and we
      cover them identically.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>No affiliate link ever appears inside a technical instruction.</strong> If a guide
      tells you to install something, that link goes to the official source, always.
    </li>
    <li>
      We write the guide <strong>before</strong> anyone checks whether an affiliate programme
      exists. The commercial question is deliberately asked last.
    </li>
    <li>
      Commission rates vary between vendors and are never taken into account when choosing which
      tool to write a guide about, or what that guide says.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="what-we-refuse">What we refuse to do</h2>
  <p>
    This list exists so you can hold us to it. We do not:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Publish sponsored guides, paid tutorials, or vendor-written copy under our byline</li>
    <li>Sell placement in a guide, or a "featured partner" slot</li>
    <li>Steer a reader toward a paid tier that the task does not require</li>
    <li>Change what a guide says because a vendor asked, complained, or threatened</li>
    <li>Accept payment for a link, or add an affiliate link to a tool we did not independently choose to cover</li>
    <li>Remove a criticism in exchange for anything</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Vendors get exactly one privilege: they can tell us a command is wrong or a feature was
    described incorrectly, and we will check it and fix it. That is the same privilege every
    reader has.
  </p>

  <h2 id="how-guides-work">How the guides are produced</h2>
  <p>
    Every guide follows the same seven chapters, every command is run on a clean machine before
    publication, and each chapter states the tool version it was verified against. The full
    editorial process is on our <a href="/about#how-we-write">about page</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Which tools we cover is decided by what readers ask for and by what we are using ourselves.
    It has never been decided by which vendor has an affiliate programme.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ftc">Regulatory note</h2>
  <p>
    This disclosure is made in accordance with the United States Federal Trade Commission's
    16 CFR Part 255 guidance on endorsements and testimonials, and with equivalent advertising
    standards in the EU and UK requiring commercial relationships to be disclosed clearly and
    conspicuously.
  </p>

  <h2 id="report">If you think we broke this</h2>
  <p>
    Tell us. Email <a href={`mailto:${SITE.email}`}>{SITE.email}</a> or use the
    <a href="/contact">contact form</a> and select "Report a factual error". If you are right, we
    publish the correction and say what happened. A disclosure policy nobody can challenge is
    decoration.
  </p>
</LegalLayout>
